## The constructions

**One family of objects.** Works identified by their content; references between them that carry credit; events on clocks. Every experience below is these three with a permission set.

**The room** (the synchronized stem players). Shared: the work (the same stems, by content), the clock (a beat grid; playing together needs alignment within tens of milliseconds, solved at room scale by tempo-and-phase sync of the Ableton Link kind), and each person's events (what they did, never the audio). Each device renders the whole room from the shared work, the shared clock and everyone's events. No master mix, no host; a late joiner takes the event history; a dropped device leaves the room playing. Who may send events that change the mix decides the experience: one person (a radio station, an artist's station, a listening party), everyone on their own layer (a band). A contribution is a credited layer: the talent bar at zero.

**The wave** (Crowdsurf). A surf references the song by content and the surf it came from; the wave is the tree those references form. Full credit without decay is the correct rule: everyone on the path from the root to a listener is a necessary cause of that arrival. Credit is reachability. The Indra's net interface is the same fact shown: the connections are the data.

**The work and its making** (the DAW). Immutable content-addressed objects give the full edit history at the cost of the changes only. Producers wire their own flows from typed parts, checked as they are joined. A synthesizer is a small system of differential equations: a patch is a typed program, recomposable, provable, shared like a stem.

**The station and the tip.** The station plays one song to everyone at one moment, with the listeners present to each other. A tip is an event on the room's clock: it lands at the moment of the music it answers, so the artist sees what moved whom. Tipping is the business model Crowdsurf lacked; the wave says who brought each tipper.

**The evaluation loop** (Matt Wang). Dynamic time warping returns a warping path, a monotone correspondence between performance and score: identity as a path with its witness, carrying where the player rushed, dragged or dropped. A piano battle is two performances whose paths anyone can check.

**Papers and comments.** Reading a paper is a surf of the paper; a lab's reading graph is a wave; notes attach to exact spans. A reply references the claim it answers, not the message before it; two who disagree hold two constructions over shared premises (Part II, “Communication”).

