## Insight not yet expressed (Claude's, reached from the above)

**The sample graph and the wave are one credit structure in two directions.** The sample graph credits backward in time: what a work is built from. The wave credits outward through people: who carried it to whom. A work's complete credit is both. So a tip need not stop at the artist: routed down the sample graph, a fan's love for "Through the Wire" reaches Chaka Khan and the writers of "Through the Fire" in the same moment. That is "the future serves the past just as the past serves the future" as a payment rule, and it is the answer to Gregory Coleman: the Amen break is paid every time anyone loves anything built on it.

**Credit transports along paths.** A live take in a room relates to the studio work by a witnessed path (the warping path of the evaluation loop, plus the layers added). Because identity is a path and not a filename, whatever is owed to the work is owed along the path to the performance and back: credit is transported the way the paper transports structure along equivalences. A remix, a cover and a live version are the work up to a path, credited as such.

**There is no artist/fan duality because a surf and a layer are the same kind of object.** Both are a reference with credit: one adds a carrier, the other adds a sound. The first person to surf an unknown artist is credited, by the no-decay rule, for everyone who comes after: the A&R role of a label, held by everyone and paid to whoever had the taste first. Talent is revealed by which layers rooms keep and which surfs spread, a measurement no gatekeeper holds. That is the decentralized tastemaking of the 11:54 message, made exact.

